Z||000000||newfie||04-24-2000||09:11 AM||||Thanks for the people who replied to my previous Q's.  I was really suprised at the amount of info out there.  As I said before I am currently lookinig for my first Fiero.  It seems to me like the 88 GT is the car of choice.  Where can I find some performance numbers on the car.  Also where Fieros build from the factoy with the removable roof or is that an aftermarket thing.  Also what about fibreglass bodies?  Whats the deal with the V8 conversion?  Will it actually fit? If so does it decrease handling? Can the axles handle V8 power?  Would it be better just to beef up the v6?  What is different about the 88 from other years?  <BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||04-24-2000||09:24 AM||||For V-8 questions check out:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com" TARGET=_blank>www.v8archie.com</A> <P>and check out the FAQ section.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||04-24-2000||11:33 AM||||Okay, I'll try...<BR>The 88 suspension was redesigned completely. Was more "purpose built", where the 84-87 suspension was assembled from existing designs. 84-87s used Chevette front suspension pieces and Citation pieces in the rear (actually the entire X-car front end, more or less, was installed in the rear of the Fiero). There are varying opinions on how much difference the redesign made, but the general consensus is that there was a big improvement in handling and stability. The 88 GT is the most sought-after, and also the most expensive. The Formula was produced in 88 only, and has all of the GT engine and suspension stuff, but fewer standard "creature comforts" (power accessories and such). Formulas could be ordered with any option except subwoofer systems and power mirrors.  <BR>Removeable sunroofs were available from the factory in all years. T-Tops were available as an aftermarket add-on from "Cars & Concepts". The 88's actually added a GM RPO code for the T-Tops, and they could be ordered through the dealer as a production option. C&C still did the work, though. <BR>4-cyl to bigger engine swaps are fairly common. The 84's are more difficult to convert to a V-6, since a V-6 was not available in 84. Lots of stuff was redesigned in 85 to accommodate the V-6. This becomes less important (I think) if you are going to do a V-8. <BR>Hope this helps, some.   ||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||04-24-2000||06:42 PM||||Not to nit-pick, but I believe the 88 suspension wasn't a redesign as much as it was a return to the original design. This design was removed and not tooled after the board pointed out that the cost of the base car as substanitally higher than what was presented to them to get the project approved. Something about having to get the base down into the $6k range. The Pontaic T1000 and X-body front suspension designed was swapped in.<P>However, and IMHO, of the 88's I've driven, a rebuilt 84-87 suspension performs to at least 90% of the 88. The difference is there, but not like going from a VW to a Porsche. Still feels like a Fiero. But if you can find one, they are nicer and more expensive to own.||||reg||1
